<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.3.4: Using Function Notation with a Real-World Example</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00888</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.3.4: Using Function Notation with a Real-World Example</md:title>
    <md:uuid>f6a358f4-ea3b-42ba-b602-8f0f01dda140</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001"><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">An art museum opens at 9 a.m. and closes at 5 p.m. The function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>V</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> gives the number of visitors in the museum <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> hours after it opens.</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">1. Explain what this statement tells us about the situation: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>V</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1.25</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>28</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00004">An hour and 15 minutes after the museum opened (at 10:15 a.m.), there were 28 visitors in the building.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">2. Use function notation to represent each statement:</para>
        <para id="para-00006">a. At 1 p.m., there were 257 visitors in the museum.</para>
      
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00008"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>V</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>257</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">b. At the time of closing, there were no visitors in the museum.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00011"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>V</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
          <para id="para-00012">3. Use the previous statements about the visitors in the museum to sketch a graph that could represent the function.
          </para>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00013">When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00014">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00015">
                      <media alt="Function. hours after opening time and number of visitors.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_06_09_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00016">Your graph should show points at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1.25</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>28</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>257</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, with or without lines connecting these points.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>
  </content>
</document>